  { q: 'Are ravens really bad luck?', a: 'No single answer applies — it depends on the tradition. In Norse mythology, ravens were associated with Odin\'s wisdom, not misfortune. In many Indigenous cultures of the Pacific Northwest, the Raven is a creator and trickster figure. The "bad luck" association is strongest in some European Christian traditions where ravens were linked to death and battle, but even there the association was contextual, not universal.' },
  { q: 'What does it mean when a raven visits you?', a: 'There is no scientifically meaningful answer to this question — birds do not deliver omens. What a raven visiting means culturally varies enormously by tradition. If the question is about behaviour: ravens are curious and intelligent birds that may approach humans in search of food or out of exploratory interest.' },
  { q: 'Why are ravens associated with death?', a: 'Ravens are carrion birds — they eat dead animals and historically gathered at battlefields and places of execution. This observable behaviour made them a natural symbol for death and the battlefield in many cultures independently. The association is rooted in real raven ecology, not superstition.' },
];
---

